    "id": "black-hole-primary-star-system",
    "title": "A black hole in star slot 1 makes the whole system a black-hole system - the PRIMARY slot decides the starbase anchor, the galaxy-map icon, the lighting source and every body's orbit centre, and a black hole LIGHTS its system like a star",
    "category": "script",
    "summary": "Star slot ORDER is the whole mechanism of a binary star class, and slot 1 is the PRIMARY. The probe <mods>/zz_probe_accretion put pc_black_hole in slot 1 and pc_g_star in slot 2 of sc_accretion and launched clean, then the USER LOOKED. What the on-screen verdict established: a black hole primary plus a yellow companion RENDERS - a pairing with ZERO vanilla precedent, so \"untested by the install\" was not \"impossible\"; the system is LIT and the colonisable world is BRIGHT, exactly as gfx/lights/star_lights.asset:231/239 predicts, because black_hole_light has intensity 2.5, the same as g_class_star at :129; the view is slightly dim/tinted, which is the black-hole colour LUT and galaxy_background_hsv_shift of gfx/worldgfx/star_black_hole.txt:12/19 doing their job; the home starbase sits on the BLACK HOLE, because every outpost's potential_construction demands is_primary_star (common/ship_sizes/00_starbases.txt:86-92); the galaxy map shows a black-hole system although sc_accretion declares NO icon; and a colonisable body placed BETWEEN the two stars by a negative relative orbit_distance (150 + -75 = 75) still ORBITS THE PRIMARY - there is no barycentric two-star orbit for bodies. The design consequence: an origin that wants its habitable world to appear to orbit the YELLOW star must put the yellow star in slot 1 and the black hole in slot 2.",
