    "rules": [
      "An empire's second system can only come from the initializer tree, and the neighbor_system block MUST carry a distance = { min max }. An origin names exactly one initializer; the tree's neighbor_system + mandatory_neighbors = yes is the only route to a different system. Without the distance limits the engine logs prescripted_systems.cpp:795: Mandatory neighbor_system for initializer <x> has min distance 0 at load and the fallback would place the new system at distance 0 from its parent. Documented at example.txt:154-157.",
      "An origin's initializer CANNOT create a colony: there is no country scope during origin galaxy-gen. 01_start_of_game_effects.txt:1 says why - initializers run *\"before the empire actually exists\"*. Put the colony in on_game_start_country (common/on_actions/00_on_actions.txt:31), which takes an events list and nothing else.",
      "Every scripted colony must seed population, or it reverts to a colonisable planet with NO log line. create_colony builds the colony object alone. The shape to copy is the Toxic Knights' (origin_toxic_knights.txt:64-78): create_colony, then change_colony_foundation_date, then while = { count = 8 create_pop_group = { size = 100 species = <owner main species> } }. This is a silent failure, so it must be checked rather than hoped for - see create-colony-without-pops in this plugin's rule table.",
      "Ownership of a system IS its primary starbase; a colony gives you nothing territorial. Give the second system an outpost (create_starbase = { size = starbase_outpost owner = root }, legal in planet and galactic_object scope) or the colony sits in an unowned system with no border. A second-system colony WITHOUT an outpost is an exposed enclave.",
      "Never assert has_owner in the same effect block that creates the starbase. Vanilla's own comment - federations_initializers.txt:963 *\"needs delay for system ownership to settle\"* - is the rule, and the probe reproduced it from the other side (LONGROAD-S-EVENT-STARBASE-DONE then LONGROAD-OWN-AFTER-STARBASE-UNOWNED). Re-check from a deferred event (days = 1 or more), or read a save.",
      "starting_planet = yes is honoured only inside the initializer the empire actually USED. A body carrying it in a neighbor_system initializer is IGNORED (LONGROAD-A-ENGINE-DID-NOT-COLONISE-PROBE-BODY). Do not expect the engine to colonise the second system for you - this is precisely why the on_game_start_country half exists.",
      "The initializer tree's hyperlane is what connects the two systems. hyperlane_jumps = { min = 1 max = 1 } on the neighbor_system block produced exactly one jump (LONGROAD-C-REACHABLE-HYPERLANE-YES), which is what makes the second colony reachable rather than merely owned. If the tree ever fails to produce a neighbour, the fallback is spawn_system from a country event at game start (common/scripted_effects/01_start_of_game_effects.txt:3915-3923, origin_spawn_system_effect), which vanilla uses for Imperial Fiefdom and Arc Welders.",
      "Expect the name-list renaming to reach the NEIGHBOUR system too, and repair it from a game-start event. README_NAME_LISTS.txt:7 only promises it for the home system, but LONGROAD-NAME-BEFORE-colony1-北方乐土 and ...-probe-伊尔巴II show the second system renamed as well. set_name repairs it - and also overwrites the player's capital name if pointed at the capital, which is a visible change and belongs in a checklist, not in a silent fix.",
      "There is no vanilla example of the whole recipe, so the parts must be measured separately. The player-empire two-system start has zero precedent; each mechanism above has a vanilla precedent of its own (the neighbour tree: Void Dwellers; the outpost in a neighbour: Lost Colony; the seeded colony: Toxic Knights), and it was combining them that had never been done."
    ],
    "breaks": [
      "Do NOT try to colonise from inside the origin's initializer. The scope is galactic_object (example.txt:52) and there is no country yet; a guarded random_country = { limit = { ... } } block simply never matches (LONGROAD-B-INIT-NO-COUNTRY-SCOPE). It is not a syntax problem and no error is raised - the block is well-formed and inert. The same idiom in a misc_system_init initializer works, which is exactly the trap: copying the Lost Colony code into an origin initializer produces a colony that silently never exists.",
      "Do NOT rely on starting_planet = yes to colonise a neighbour. It is honoured in the initializer the empire uses and IGNORED in a neighbor_system one. The probe put the field on a deliberately separate body so this could be measured without endangering the real colony, and the engine did not colonise it.",
      "Do NOT read \"the OLD engine makes a colony claim its system\" into create_colony. It does not: LONGROAD-OWN-AFTER-COLONY-UNOWNED was checked before the starbase existed. If a second-system colony looks fine on the map, that is the OUTPOST working, not the colony.",
      "Do NOT conclude the outpost failed because has_owner is still no. Ownership does not settle in-tick - vanilla says so and the probe reproduced it. The user's on-screen confirmation (\"两个殖民地，两个恒星系\") is what settles that the outpost DID claim the system; the in-tick log check could only ever say \"not yet\", and reading it as a failure would have thrown away a working mechanism.",
      "Do NOT skip the population and hope. A zero-population colony reverts to a colonisable planet with no log line and no error - the defect is invisible in every log the engine writes, and it was found by reading a save, not a log. Any scripted colony without create_pop_group in its chain is a defect waiting for the first unpause.",
      "Do NOT forget that create_starbase is only legal in planet and galactic_object scope (effects.log). It works mid-game as well as at start - vanilla uses it for rebels (events/unrest_events.txt:1715), for the Scion master (events/origin_events_1.txt:1641) and for Synthetic Dawn (events/synthetic_dawn_events.txt:1967) - so a \"give the player a second system later\" design does not need galaxy generation at all.",
      "\"No vanilla origin does this, so it is unsupported\" is wrong as a limit. Zero precedent in the install means zero precedent. The user's verdict is the counter-example: the recipe works, it is simply not something vanilla ever needed."
    ],
